  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Recharging Advocacy for Rights inEurope3</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over the past four years, due to Oxfam Novib&#x2019;s generous support, Recharging Advocacy for Rights in Europe (RARE) has strengthened the skills and solidarity networks of human rights defenders (HRDs). The second cohort (2022-24) which graduated in June 2024 came from 17 different EU Member States and represented NGOs working on a variety of cross-cutting human rights issues, from refugee rightsto digital and women&#x2019;s rights. Their skills have increased in numerous high-demand areas, including open-source investigations, communications, non-profit management, leadership, and advocacy. While they may come from different countries and organisations workingon different issues, RARE has turned them into a tightly-knit community of professionals and has empowered them to join forces to tackle threats to civic space, human rights, and the rule of law.
As part of RARE2, we investigated interaction levels amongst organisations pre- and post-RARE involvement, using an Organizational Network Analysis to display collaboration patterns. It em- ploys nodes (organisations) and edges (connections) to visualize relationships, with node size indicating influence. The comparison reveals qualitative shifts resulting from organizations&apos; engage- ment in RARE, as detailed in the table showcasing influence, communication intensity, and network changes. The detailed ONA report can be viewed here.
In the process of strengthening this pan-European alliance of rule of law defenders, RARE has also developed a profile of its own, helping to drive conversations on civic space issues amongcivil society actors and policy circles in the EU.
2. RARE3 (2024-2026)
Following two successful cohorts, we are expanding RARE&apos;s geographic and topical scope. Beyond HRDs from EUMS, for its 2024-2026 cohort, RARE has recruited four of its 25 Following two successful cohorts, we are expanding RARE&apos;s geographic and topical scope.
Beyond HRDs from EUMS, for its 2024-2026 cohort, RARE has recruited four of its 25
participants from Ukraine, Moldova and the Western Balkans, as many HRDs from these countries are seeking safety or already residing in the EU.
Several RARE participants in the first two cohorts came from organisations already supported by OxfamNovib, a trend that continues in RARE3.</narrative>
  </description>
